-# Phase 3 — Conditional Workflow Runtime (Graph Engine)
-
-Phase 3 turns the existing fixed mission runner into a bounded graph control
-runtime without allowing a model to emit executable code or arbitrary graph
-handlers.
-
-## Execution modes
-
-- `legacy`: existing runner only.
-- `shadow`: compile the graph, create a durable GraphRun, execute deterministic
-  control nodes, and stop before the first agent, fan-out, approval, or effect
-  boundary. The existing runner still produces the mission result.
-- `conditional`: execute the typed graph under the MissionRun lease.
-
-Start in `shadow` on staging. Promote only low-risk missions to `conditional`
-after PostgreSQL/PGMQ crash-and-resume tests pass.
-
-## Default graph
-
-```text
-budget_guard -> triage
-  simple GREEN + reversible -> direct -> verify
-  otherwise -> research_dispatch -> research[1..N] -> research_join
-            -> plan -> risk_gate -> approval? -> execute -> verify
-
-verify passed -> synthesize -> verify_final -> final_gate -> finalize
-verify or verify_final failed -> diagnose -> research | plan | execute | verify | human | stop
-```
-
-The verifier returns a structured Failure Packet. Its proposed route is treated
-as untrusted input; deterministic policy computes the actual route from the
-failure type, stable fingerprint, occurrence count, severity, retryability,
-risk, and remaining budget.
-
-## Safety properties
-
-The GraphSpec compiler rejects:
-
-- unregistered handlers, predicates, policies, and projectors;
-- a handler whose node kind, role, input projector, output schema, or tool policy
-  differs from its registered contract;
-- any success path that bypasses an independent verifier;
-- verifier edges that can reach success without the registered
-  `verification_passed` predicate or a fresh verifier;
-- any mutation-capable node placed after verification without another independent
-  verifier before success;
-- any success path that bypasses the final gate, or a final-gate edge that can
-  reach success without `final_gate_passed`;
-- irreversible nodes reachable without an approval dominator, including rejected
-  or unconditional approval edges that can reach an irreversible action;
-- fan-out without a detached child and join node;
-- verifier or researcher privilege escalation;
-- every cycle that can avoid a registered bounded retry edge;
-- limits above the hard fan-out, parallelism, transition, and iteration caps.
-
-Node results are canonicalized before use. Accessors, prototype keys, symbols,
-cycles, non-finite numbers, oversized payloads, extra result fields, invalid
-usage, malformed Failure Packets, and forbidden state deltas are rejected. Each
-handler also has an allowlist of state-delta fields, so a valid role cannot use
-an otherwise valid result envelope to smuggle route, approval, or final status.
-
-## Persistence
-
-Phase 3 adds:
-
-- `GraphDefinition`: immutable versioned GraphSpec and policy hash;
-- `GraphRun`: durable state, selected node, limits, and fixed-precision budget;
-- `GraphNodeRun`: role-scoped input, output, usage, and attempt history;
-- `GraphCheckpoint`: resumable state at node/super-step boundaries;
-- `GraphEvent`: ordered route and policy audit trail;
-- `FailurePacket`: structured, fingerprinted verification failures;
-- `GraphBudgetEntry`: reserve/release/actual ledger in integer micros.
-
-All graph mutations require the current MissionRun owner, lease epoch, and
-unexpired lease. During fan-out, every successful branch is settled before a
-sibling error is propagated, so consumed calls, tokens, cost, and evidence are
-never erased by partial branch failure. A replacement worker abandons stale active
-node attempts, releases their outstanding reservations with idempotent ledger entries, updates
-the state budget, and writes a recovery checkpoint before creating a new
-attempt. Graph failure also closes every active reservation and persists a
-failed checkpoint.
-
-Every resume and read verifies the canonical state hash. The runtime mode,
-GraphSpec hash, and policy version are pinned when the GraphRun starts and are
-checked on subsequent execution. A deployment or database mutation that changes
-one of those values fails closed instead of silently changing the route of an
-in-flight run.
-
-A passing verifier pins a verification target hash over the objective,
-requirements, constraints, implementation artifacts, synthesized artifact,
-mission outcome, verification profile, and approval scope. Synthesis is followed
-by a separate `verify_final` node, so agent-generated final packaging cannot be
-promoted solely because the pre-synthesis implementation passed. The Final Gate
-recomputes the target hash; any post-verification artifact, outcome,
-policy-relevant, or approval change forces the graph back through verification.
-
-## Human approval
-
-Approval is a graph node, not an exception path. A decision contains a scope
-hash bound to the graph spec hash, policy version, node, mission, risk,
-reversibility, plan artifact, and maximum cost. Resolution requires the exact
-hash, an active CEO principal, pending status, and non-expired decision.
-Approval pauses do not consume the MissionRun retry budget. An approval that
-expires is atomically resolved as `expired`; its node, checkpoint, GraphRun, and
-MissionRun are committed as terminal `partial` before the API returns 409. The
-mission reconciler also processes expired approvals in bounded batches, so an
-unattended approval does not remain paused indefinitely. Graph decisions can
-only be resolved through the graph-scoped endpoint; the generic legacy decision
-endpoint rejects them to prevent scope checks from being bypassed.
-
-## Role projections
-
-- Router: classification, route, budget, iteration, failure summaries.
-- Researcher: objective, research questions, source policy, branch budget, and
-  structured open Failure Packets when routed back for an evidence gap.
-- Planner: requirements, verified research references, and structured open
-  Failure Packets when routed back for a design error.
-- Specialist: plan, relevant evidence, open Failure Packets, approval status.
-- Verifier: requirements, implementation artifacts, prior verification refs,
-  open Failure Packets, mission outcome, and tests; producer self-narrative and
-  research narrative are excluded.
-- Human: risk, impact scope, plan, failures, approval, budget.
-- Synthesizer: verified artifacts and verification result references only.
-
-## API
-
-```text
-GET  /api/company/runs/:missionRunId/graph
-POST /api/company/graph-decisions/:decisionId
-POST /api/company/graphs/validate
-```
-
-The existing organization-scoped authorization layer protects all endpoints.
-Viewer responses omit raw node inputs/outputs, budget ledger metadata, and
-full policy payloads.
-
-## Validation
-
-```bash
-npm run graph:validate-default
-npm run test:phase3:core
-npm run test:phase3:postgres
-npm run check
-npm test
-npm run build
-```
-
-`test:phase3:postgres` applies all three Supabase migrations and validates
-scope-bound approval, approval replay prevention, automatic approval expiry,
-retry neutralization, database budget limits, stale lease rejection, crash
-recovery, and checkpoint uniqueness.
-
-## Known boundary
-
-The default role handlers are safe adapters around the existing mission runner.
-Research nodes record synthetic delegation artifacts until a live research
-provider is connected. Direct and work-execution handlers adapt the existing
-whole-mission runner rather than running independent live specialist models.
-Model class selection is persisted and audited, but the existing provider
-adapters must be updated separately to consume it. The runtime therefore
-provides conditional routing, durability, isolation, and bounded adaptivity; it
-does not yet generate arbitrary GraphSpecs, execute model-authored JavaScript,
-or claim that the placeholder role adapters are independent live agents.
